Why the second commit exists.

The issue scoped the guarded write actions as stage, unstage, commit, branch,
checkout, merge, cherry-pick, stash and stash pop. The API implemented all
of them from the start, but only stage, stash, merge and commit had anywhere
in the interface to be reached from, so branch creation and cherry-picking
were features that existed in the backend and nowhere else.

The branch-name validation is worth a second look, because it is the one
place where a browser-supplied string comes close to a command line. The
allow-list regex rejects anything that is not a plain branch name: shell
metacharacters, a leading dash that git would read as an option, and path
traversal. Verified against 'main; rm -rf /', '--upload-pack=evil', '-x' and
'../../etc' - all rejected before anything was run. The action array is built
from values that have already passed that check, and git is invoked through
spawn with an argument array rather than a shell, so there is no second
parsing pass to get wrong.
